"""34 concept-file entries for the Apex-Reality Computer databases.

Each entry: (group, title, description, key_points[4], requirements, demo_key).
Honesty: every description and how-it-works is written as SPECULATIVE CONCEPT.
"""
CC = "core-concepts"
SI = "systems-integration"
AE = "apex-engineering"

ENTRIES = [
(CC, "The JAH-UALNS Number Line",
 "The JAH-UALNS number line is the Signature concept of a universal measuring line: every reading the Apex-Reality Computer takes - plasma flux, light coherence, void depth, bridge tension - is mapped to one shared line from ORIGIN (0.0) to APEX (1.0), with named anchors at HARMONY (0.25), THRESHOLD (0.5), and RADIANCE (0.75). It is a design concept for unifying incompatible measurements, not a discovered law of nature.",
 ["Every measurement becomes a position between 0.0 (ORIGIN) and 1.0 (APEX), so unlike quantities can be compared on one line.",
  "Five named anchors - ORIGIN, HARMONY, THRESHOLD, RADIANCE, APEX - give operators plain words for positions instead of raw decimals.",
  "Mapping is linear and reversible: a position always converts back to the original reading and its unit.",
  "The line is a concept tool for the console simulator; it claims no physical reality beyond the design."],
 "A console that can do arithmetic; a list of readings with known ranges; the anchor table above. Nothing physical is required because this is a concept model.",
 "numberline"),

(CC, "JAH-UALNS: Reading Positions on the Line",
 "Reading a position on the JAH-UALNS line is the concept skill of turning a raw instrument value into a line position and then into an anchor-relative statement an operator can act on, such as 'plasma flux sits just past THRESHOLD, nearest anchor HARMONY side'. The concept defines three reading modes: absolute (the decimal position), anchor-relative (nearest named anchor and distance), and banded (which quarter of the line the reading falls in).",
 ["Absolute mode reports the decimal position, for example 0.37, for precise concept computation.",
  "Anchor-relative mode reports the nearest named anchor, for example '0.12 past THRESHOLD', for human operators.",
  "Banded mode reports the quarter - origin, harmony, threshold, or radiance band - for quick triage.",
  "All three modes are reversible to the original reading, so no information is lost in the concept mapping."],
 "The anchor table; readings with documented ranges; a parser that formats the three modes. Concept-only: no hardware.",
 "numberline"),

(CC, "The AUA-JM2 Protocol",
 "The AUA-JM2 protocol is the Signature concept of a meaning-first message protocol for the Apex-Reality systems: every message is framed with STX/ETX markers, carries sender, sequence number, and payload, and is sealed with a checksum, so the console, the swarm, and the bridge can exchange commands that are verifiably intact. A three-step handshake - HELLO, SYNC, READY - establishes that both ends agree on the concept session before any command flows.",
 ["Framing uses STX (start) and ETX (end) markers so a receiver can find message boundaries in a stream.",
  "Each frame carries sender identity, a sequence number for ordering, and a checksum that detects corruption.",
  "The HELLO/SYNC/READY handshake means no commands are accepted from an unsynchronized concept peer.",
  "The protocol is a design for the concept simulator; it is not a deployed real-world standard."],
 "A text channel between concept components; sequence counters; the checksum rule (sum of bytes mod 256).",
 "protocol"),

(CC, "AUA-JM2 Message Framing",
 "Message framing under AUA-JM2 is the concept rulebook for packing a command into bytes: sender name, four-digit sequence, payload text, and a two-hex-digit checksum, wrapped in STX/ETX. The concept also defines the failure behaviors: a bad checksum means the frame is dropped and re-requested, an unknown sender means the frame is quarantined, and a duplicate sequence number means the frame is acknowledged but not re-executed.",
 ["The frame layout is fixed-order: sender, sequence, payload, checksum - so parsers stay trivial.",
  "Checksums are verified before any payload is trusted, which is the concept's integrity guarantee.",
  "Duplicate sequence numbers are acknowledged without re-execution, giving at-most-once concept semantics.",
  "Framing rules exist so the console simulator behaves deterministically; they standardize nothing in the real world."],
 "The framing layout documented above; a parser implementing the three failure behaviors.",
 "protocol"),

(CC, "Concept: Signature-Stamped Authority",
 "Signature-stamped authority is the branding concept of the A7 Signature Dominion: every Apex-Reality concept file carries the mark of its designer, Justin Addam Higgins, so that anyone encountering the design knows whose imagination it came from and under what standing rules it was made - honest labeling, no real-world claims, speculative throughout. The concept defines the stamp as a signature block, not a legal instrument.",
 ["The stamp names the designer and the dominion, binding the concept to its declared fictional frame.",
  "Stamped files repeat the honesty rule: speculative design, never presented as proven fact.",
  "The stamp travels with derivatives - blueprints, personas, and registry entries all carry it.",
  "It is a branding and honesty device for a concept universe, not a certification of anything real."],
 "The signature block text; the honesty rule repeated on every file; a registry of stamped works.",
 "sheet"),

(CC, "Plasma Nanobot Systems",
 "Plasma nanobot systems are the Signature concept of swarms of microscopic machines imagined to ride and shape plasma flows: in the concept, they sense flux density, align into lattices that channel the flow, and report their positions back over the AUA-JM2 protocol. Their concept role in the Apex-Reality Computer is energy handling - the hot, fast, high-throughput subsystem that the cooler light and void systems balance.",
 ["Each concept nanobot senses local plasma flux and shares the reading with its neighbors.",
  "Swarms align into lattice formations that, in the concept, shape flow the way a lens shapes light.",
  "Position and status reports travel over AUA-JM2 frames so the console sees one coherent swarm.",
  "Everything about plasma nanobots here is speculative concept design, not an existing technology."],
 "The swarm coordination concept; the AUA-JM2 protocol; a simulator for lattice formation. No real nanobots.",
 "swarm"),

(CC, "Light Nanobot Systems",
 "Light nanobot systems are the Signature concept of swarms imagined to manipulate coherent light: in the concept, they tune their surfaces to steer, split, and recombine beams, forming the signaling and sensing layer of the Apex-Reality Computer. Where plasma bots handle energy, light bots handle information - the concept's nervous system, carrying console commands at the speed of the design's imagination.",
 ["Concept light bots steer beams by coordinated surface tuning across the swarm.",
  "Splitting and recombining beams gives the concept its signaling alphabet.",
  "They double as sensors: beam distortion reports back as environmental readings.",
  "This is speculative concept design; no such machines exist outside the concept."],
 "The swarm coordination concept; a beam-path model for the simulator; AUA-JM2 signaling.",
 "swarm"),

(CC, "Void Nanobot Systems",
 "Void nanobot systems are the Signature concept of swarms imagined to operate in the design's 'void' - the cold, quiet substrate beneath plasma and light where the concept stores stable state. In the fiction, void bots are slow, patient, and nearly inert, forming the long-term memory lattice that the time-aware memory engine reads and writes. They are the concept's ballast against the frenzy of the other two swarms.",
 ["Void bots trade speed for stability, holding formation for long concept durations.",
  "Their lattice is the imagined physical home of the time-aware memory engine's checkpoints.",
  "They communicate rarely and in bulk, keeping the concept's quiet layer quiet.",
  "Speculative concept design throughout; the 'void' is a fiction of the design, not a place."],
 "The memory engine concept; the swarm coordination concept; a slow-update simulation loop.",
 "swarm"),

(CC, "Nanobot Swarm Coordination",
 "Swarm coordination is the concept algorithm by which the Apex-Reality nanobots - plasma, light, and void - are imagined to move as one: each bot adjusts its position a fraction of the way toward its assigned lattice target every tick, shares its position with neighbors, and the swarm converges when the spread falls below a threshold. The concept keeps the rule deliberately simple so the simulator stays honest and explainable.",
 ["Each bot moves a fixed fraction toward its target per tick - no hidden magic in the concept.",
  "Neighbor position-sharing lets the swarm detect stragglers and re-balance targets.",
  "Convergence is declared when the maximum spread drops below the concept threshold.",
  "The algorithm is a teaching model for the simulator, not a claim about real swarm robotics."],
 "A tick loop; position state per bot; the convergence threshold. Pure simulation.",
 "swarm"),

(CC, "Portal Theory: The Reality Bridge Primer",
 "Portal theory is the Signature concept of how the Apex-Reality Computer imagines crossing distances: a portal is a stabilized span between two anchored points, and the concept's energy equation - energy equals a constant times distance squared divided by stability - decides which spans are STABLE, MARGINAL, or UNSTABLE. The reality bridge is the engineered form of the theory: anchors emplaced, spans surveyed, crossings scheduled in the fiction.",
 ["The concept energy equation E = k*d^2/stability makes long or unstable spans expensive.",
  "Spans are classified STABLE, MARGINAL, or UNSTABLE against the concept energy budget.",
  "Anchors must be emplaced at both ends before any span is evaluated - the concept has no anchorless portals.",
  "This is speculative fiction mathematics; it describes the concept, not physics."],
 "The energy equation with chosen constants; an anchor list; the budget threshold.",
 "portal"),

(CC, "The Warp Console",
 "The warp console is the Signature concept of the operator's chair for the whole Apex-Reality Computer: one screen showing the JAH-UALNS line positions of every subsystem, a command line speaking AUA-JM2, harmonic stack controls for the imagined warp field, and big honest labels reminding the operator that everything here is concept simulation. In the fiction it is where a commander would sit; in reality it is the interactive demo on this database's page.",
 ["One display merges number-line positions, protocol traffic, swarm state, and bridge spans.",
  "The command line accepts concept commands - boot, status, scan, warp, help - and answers honestly.",
  "Harmonic stack controls let the operator explore the concept's coherence model.",
  "The console is an interactive concept experience, and says so on its face."],
 "The concept subsystems above; a command parser; the honesty banner. Try it on this page's Console tab.",
 "console"),

(CC, "The Time-Aware Memory Engine",
 "The time-aware memory engine is the Signature concept of a memory that never forgets when: every event is recorded with a sequence timestamp, checkpoints snapshot the whole concept state, and queries like 'what was true at time T' are answered from history rather than from current state. In the fiction it lets the Apex-Reality Computer rewind and audit itself; as a concept it is a clean teaching model of temporal databases.",
 ["Every event carries a sequence timestamp, so 'when' is a first-class concept citizen.",
  "Checkpoints snapshot full state, and restore returns the concept to that exact moment.",
  "Temporal queries ('as of time T') read history, never the live state.",
  "The engine is a concept teaching model; it makes no claim to real time travel."],
 "An event log; a checkpoint/restore mechanism; the temporal query rule.",
 "memory"),

(SI, "Plasma-Light-Void Triad Integration",
 "Triad integration is the concept design for making the three nanobot swarms - plasma (energy), light (signal), void (memory) - work as one machine instead of three: plasma feeds the concept energy budget, light carries the concept command traffic, and void holds the concept state, with the warp console arbitrating when the swarms' needs conflict. The concept's rule is priority order: memory integrity first, then signal clarity, then energy throughput.",
 ["Each swarm has one concept job: plasma moves energy, light moves information, void holds state.",
  "The console arbitrates conflicts by a fixed priority: void, then light, then plasma.",
  "Cross-swarm messages travel over AUA-JM2 so all three speak the same concept language.",
  "Integration is a systems-design concept; the swarms themselves remain speculative."],
 "All three swarm concepts; the AUA-JM2 protocol; the console arbitration rule.",
 "swarm"),

(SI, "Portal Stabilization Lattice",
 "The portal stabilization lattice is the concept structure imagined to hold a portal open: a ring of light nanobots maintaining beam geometry, plasma nanobots feeding the concept energy budget, and void nanobots anchoring the long-term shape, all coordinated through the swarm algorithm. Stability in the concept is the ratio the energy equation divides by - raise stability with a denser lattice, and farther spans become affordable.",
 ["The lattice has three rings - light for geometry, plasma for energy, void for shape memory.",
  "Stability is a concept number the operator raises by densifying the lattice.",
  "Higher stability directly lowers portal energy in the concept equation.",
  "The lattice is speculative engineering fiction, simulated here as geometry and arithmetic."],
 "The portal energy concept; the three swarm concepts; a lattice density parameter.",
 "portal"),

(SI, "Warp Field Harmonics",
 "Warp field harmonics are the concept's model of the imagined warp field as a stack of harmonics: each harmonic contributes amplitude divided by its number, phase-shifted, and the field's coherence is the average of those contributions. Operators explore the concept by adding harmonics and watching coherence move - a clean, honest piece of concept mathematics with no physical claims attached.",
 ["The field is modeled as harmonics 1 through N, each with amplitude 1/k and a phase offset.",
  "Coherence is the mean of amplitude-times-cosine-of-phase across the stack.",
  "Adding harmonics changes coherence in visible, explainable concept steps.",
  "This is concept mathematics for the simulator, not a description of any real field."],
 "The harmonic series definition; a coherence readout; the console's harmonic controls.",
 "warp"),

(SI, "Reality Bridge Anchoring",
 "Reality bridge anchoring is the concept procedure for emplacing the endpoints a portal span needs: survey candidate anchor points, measure pairwise concept distances, test each pair against the feasibility rule (span must fit within max span scaled by anchor count), and commission the longest feasible pair. In the fiction, anchors are void-bot lattices sunk into the quiet substrate; in the simulator, they are coordinates with names.",
 ["Anchors are surveyed as named coordinates before any span is considered.",
  "Feasibility scales with anchor count: more anchors, longer affordable spans, by the concept rule.",
  "The longest feasible pair is commissioned; the rest stay surveyed for later.",
  "Anchoring is a concept procedure; the 'substrate' is fiction, the geometry is real arithmetic."],
 "Named anchor coordinates; the feasibility rule; the portal energy concept.",
 "bridge"),

(SI, "Console-to-Swarm Command Bus",
 "The command bus is the concept wiring between the warp console and the nanobot swarms: operator commands are framed as AUA-JM2 messages, addressed to swarm, lattice, or individual-bot scope, and the bus guarantees ordering by sequence number and at-most-once execution by duplicate suppression. In the simulator it is the reason 'form lattice' typed at the console visibly moves the concept swarm.",
 ["Commands are framed in AUA-JM2 with explicit scope: swarm, lattice, or bot.",
  "Sequence numbers order commands; duplicates are acknowledged but never re-executed.",
  "The bus is the single path from console intent to swarm motion in the concept.",
  "It is a simulator design pattern, honestly labeled, not a real control system."],
 "The AUA-JM2 protocol; the swarm coordination concept; the console parser.",
 "protocol"),

(SI, "Memory Engine Checkpoints",
 "Checkpoints are the concept mechanism by which the time-aware memory engine saves restorable moments: a checkpoint captures the full concept state - swarm positions, portal stabilities, bridge spans, console mode - under a timestamp, and restore returns every subsystem to exactly that captured moment. The concept's discipline is checkpoint-before-risk: no warp trial, portal test, or lattice change runs without a fresh checkpoint.",
 ["A checkpoint captures every subsystem's concept state under one timestamp.",
  "Restore is total: the concept returns to the captured moment with nothing left over.",
  "Checkpoint-before-risk is the concept's safety discipline for all trials.",
  "Checkpoints simulate perfectly because the concept state is data; no real time is involved."],
 "The memory engine concept; serializable subsystem state; the checkpoint discipline.",
 "memory"),

(SI, "AUA-JM2 over the Number Line",
 "This concept file describes addressing AUA-JM2 messages by JAH-UALNS line position instead of by name: a message addressed to '0.50' reaches whatever subsystem currently sits at THRESHOLD, which lets the console command roles ('whoever holds the bridge') rather than individuals. The concept includes the resolution rule - positions resolve at send time - and the honesty note that role-addressing is a fiction convenience, not a real routing breakthrough.",
 ["Messages can address a line position ('0.50') instead of a named subsystem.",
  "Positions resolve to subsystems at send time, so roles can move between holders.",
  "Role-addressing lets the console command 'the bridge holder' without knowing who that is.",
  "The concept is a simulator addressing mode; it routes nothing in the real world."],
 "The JAH-UALNS line; the AUA-JM2 protocol; the position-resolution rule.",
 "protocol"),

(SI, "Failure Modes and Safe Shutdown",
 "Failure modes and safe shutdown is the concept safety file: it enumerates what can go wrong in the fiction - swarm divergence, portal instability, bridge anchor loss, memory checkpoint corruption, protocol desynchronization - and defines the safe shutdown sequence that parks every subsystem in order (swarms to lattice rest, portals collapsed gracefully, bridge spans released, memory checkpointed, console to standby). The concept treats safety as a designed behavior, and the simulator implements the sequence exactly.",
 ["Five concept failure modes are enumerated with their visible signatures.",
  "Safe shutdown parks subsystems in a fixed order so nothing is left half-moved.",
  "Every risky concept trial begins with a fresh checkpoint, per the safety discipline.",
  "Safety here is a designed simulator behavior; it protects no real hardware."],
 "All subsystem concepts; the checkpoint mechanism; the shutdown order list.",
 "console"),

(SI, "The A7 Dominion Registry of Systems",
 "The A7 Dominion Registry of Systems is the concept ledger of every Apex-Reality subsystem: each system gets a unique name, a kind, and a registry ID like JAH-A7-SYS-0001, and the registry refuses duplicates so the fiction stays consistent. It is the concept's single source of truth for 'what exists' - consulted by the console, the blueprints, and the Federation layer whenever a system must be named unambiguously.",
 ["Every concept system holds exactly one registry entry with a unique name and ID.",
  "Duplicate names are refused at registration, keeping the fiction consistent.",
  "Lookups are case-insensitive so 'aua-jm2 hub' finds 'AUA-JM2 Hub'.",
  "The registry is a fiction-keeping device, honestly labeled, not a real authority."],
 "The uniqueness rule; the ID format; a lookup routine. Pure concept data.",
 "registry"),

(SI, "Operator Training Ladder",
 "The operator training ladder is the concept curriculum for learning the Apex-Reality Computer: rung one reads the JAH-UALNS line, rung two frames AUA-JM2 messages, rung three coordinates a swarm, rung four evaluates a portal span, rung five anchors a bridge, rung six checkpoints memory, and rung seven runs the full console. Each rung is a concept exercise with a visible success criterion, and the ladder is the teaching path this very database follows.",
 ["Seven rungs run from reading the number line to running the full console.",
  "Each rung has a visible success criterion checkable in the simulator.",
  "Rungs build on each other: no portal spans before swarm coordination.",
  "The ladder is a teaching design for the concept; it certifies nothing real."],
 "The subsystem concepts in ladder order; the console simulator; success criteria per rung.",
 "sheet"),

(SI, "Concept Ethics: Honest Labeling Standard",
 "The honest labeling standard is the concept's ethical core, and Manon's standing rule: every Apex-Reality file, demo, blueprint, and persona carries the speculative-concept label; nothing is presented as proven fact, real hardware, or a real starship; and the label travels with derivatives so the fiction can never quietly become a claim. This file exists so the standard itself is archived as a first-class concept.",
 ["Every file carries the speculative-concept label where it cannot be missed.",
  "The label travels with derivatives: blueprints, personas, registry entries, demos.",
  "No concept output may imply real-world capability, institutions, or hardware.",
  "The standard is enforced by screening every record before it enters the archive."],
 "The label text; screening of all records; the signature-stamp convention.",
 "sheet"),

(AE, "Apex Core: The Central Concept Processor",
 "The Apex Core is the concept of the Apex-Reality Computer's central processor: the imagined component that reads all JAH-UALNS line positions, routes all AUA-JM2 traffic, arbitrates the three swarms, and keeps the memory engine's clock - the single point where the whole fiction comes together. In the simulator it is the main loop; in the fiction it is the machine's heart. Both are labeled concept throughout.",
 ["The core reads every subsystem's line position each tick, keeping one coherent picture.",
  "It routes protocol traffic and arbitrates swarm conflicts by the fixed priority rule.",
  "It owns the memory engine's clock, so 'when' is consistent across the fiction.",
  "The core is the simulator's main loop wearing fiction's clothes - labeled as such."],
 "The subsystem concepts; the arbitration rule; a main-loop implementation.",
 "console"),

(AE, "Warp Console Mk II Design Study",
 "The Warp Console Mk II is a concept design study for the next-generation operator console: a tri-pane layout (line positions, protocol traffic, swarm/bridge state), macro keys for the shutdown sequence and checkpoint commands, and a prominent honesty banner that can never be dismissed. The study documents what the Mk II would change, what it would keep, and what it would refuse to do - refuse list first, per the honest labeling standard.",
 ["Tri-pane layout keeps positions, traffic, and swarm/bridge state visible at once.",
  "Macro keys bind the shutdown sequence and checkpoint-before-risk to single actions.",
  "The honesty banner is permanent and undismissable by design.",
  "A design study only: no Mk II is built, and the study says so."],
 "The Mk I console concept; operator feedback from the fiction; the honesty standard.",
 "console"),

(AE, "Deep Portal Theory",
 "Deep portal theory extends the primer with the concept's advanced topics: chained spans (portals whose far anchor is another portal's near anchor), stability debt (the concept's idea that marginal spans accumulate risk over crossings), and the collapse cascade (what the fiction says happens when a chained span fails mid-chain). Each topic gets its concept equation, its simulator behavior, and its honesty note.",
 ["Chained spans link portals end to end; each link is evaluated by the energy equation.",
  "Stability debt accumulates per crossing on marginal spans until maintenance resets it.",
  "A collapse cascade releases chained spans in order, per the shutdown discipline.",
  "Advanced fiction mathematics; the simulator implements it, physics does not contain it."],
 "The portal primer; the energy equation; the shutdown sequence.",
 "portal"),

(AE, "Nanobot Genesis Forge (Concept)",
 "The nanobot genesis forge is the concept of where the fiction's nanobots come from: an imagined facility that 'grows' plasma, light, and void bots to specification - lattice role, protocol address, and swarm allegiance stamped at genesis. The concept file covers the forge's imagined pipeline (pattern, charge, release), its quality rites, and the honesty note that no forge exists and no bots are grown.",
 ["The imagined pipeline runs pattern, charge, release: role and address stamped at genesis.",
  "Each bot leaves the fiction's forge with its swarm allegiance fixed.",
  "Quality rites in the fiction reject bots that fail lattice drills.",
  "A fiction facility documented as fiction; the simulator grows bots as data."],
 "The three swarm concepts; the registry (every bot batch registered); the honesty standard.",
 "swarm"),

(AE, "The Reality Bridge Span Calculator (Concept)",
 "The span calculator is the concept tool that turns bridge anchoring into arithmetic: given named anchors with coordinates, it computes every pairwise span, tests each against the feasibility rule, and recommends the longest feasible pair with its energy cost from the portal equation. In the simulator it is the demo program; in the fiction it is the bridge engineer's slide rule.",
 ["Takes named anchor coordinates and computes all pairwise concept distances.",
  "Tests each span against the feasibility rule scaled by anchor count.",
  "Recommends the longest feasible pair and prices it with the energy equation.",
  "A concept calculator; its answers guide fiction, not engineering."],
 "Anchor coordinates; the feasibility rule; the portal energy equation.",
 "bridge"),

(AE, "Time-Aware Memory: Temporal Indexing",
 "Temporal indexing is the concept's advanced memory topic: instead of scanning the whole event log for 'what was true at time T', the engine keeps an index from timestamps to state summaries, so temporal queries answer in concept-constant time. The file covers the index structure, the checkpoint compaction rule (old checkpoints fold into summaries), and the audit view that replays any interval as a transcript.",
 ["A timestamp-to-summary index makes 'as of time T' queries concept-cheap.",
  "Checkpoint compaction folds old checkpoints into summaries without losing answers.",
  "The audit view replays any interval as a readable transcript of the fiction.",
  "Concept data structures for a concept engine; no real temporal claims."],
 "The memory engine concept; the checkpoint mechanism; an index structure.",
 "memory"),

(AE, "AUA-JM2 Protocol Extensions",
 "The protocol extensions file documents the concept's advanced AUA-JM2 features: priority lanes (command traffic outranks telemetry), bulk transfer (checkpoint snapshots move as sequenced frame trains), and role addressing by line position. Each extension gets its frame format, its fallback when the peer is basic-only, and the reminder that extensions standardize nothing beyond this concept.",
 ["Priority lanes let command frames overtake telemetry in the concept queue.",
  "Bulk transfer moves snapshots as sequenced trains with per-frame checksums.",
  "Role addressing by line position is formalized as an extension here.",
  "Extensions are fiction specification; interoperability claims stop at the simulator."],
 "The AUA-JM2 base protocol; the number-line addressing concept; frame formats.",
 "protocol"),

(AE, "Swarm-Scale Coordination Math",
 "Swarm-scale coordination math is the concept's treatment of what changes when the swarm grows from twelve bots to twelve thousand: the per-tick fraction rule still converges but slower, neighbor-sharing becomes neighborhood sampling, and the console addresses lattices instead of bots. The file works the concept arithmetic - convergence steps versus swarm size - and shows why the fiction caps operational swarms where the simulator stays legible.",
 ["Convergence steps grow with swarm size under the fixed-fraction rule; the math is shown.",
  "Neighbor-sharing becomes neighborhood sampling past concept scale thresholds.",
  "The console addresses lattices, not bots, at scale - hierarchy as a concept tool.",
  "Concept scaling analysis; real swarm robotics makes its own claims elsewhere."],
 "The swarm coordination concept; the convergence rule; scale parameters.",
 "swarm"),

(AE, "The Dominion Charter (A7 Governance Concept)",
 "The Dominion Charter is the concept governance document of the A7 Signature Dominion: it declares the dominion a fiction of honest speculation, vests design authority in Justin Addam Higgins, binds all dominion works to the honest labeling standard, and defines the registry as the dominion's memory. It is world-building with an ethics clause - the fiction governs itself by the same honesty it preaches.",
 ["Declares the dominion a fiction of honest speculation, openly and first.",
  "Vests concept design authority in its named designer.",
  "Binds every dominion work to the honest labeling standard.",
  "A governance fiction that exists to keep the fiction honest."],
 "The honest labeling standard; the system registry; the signature-stamp convention.",
 "charter" if False else "sheet"),

(AE, "Apex-Reality Console: Full Operator Manual",
 "The full operator manual is the concept's complete guide to sitting at the warp console: startup ritual, reading the JAH-UALNS board, framing AUA-JM2 commands, running swarm drills, evaluating portal spans, anchoring bridges, checkpointing memory, and executing the safe shutdown - each procedure with its concept steps and its success check. It is the teaching ladder written as one continuous fiction-manual, and the manual never lets the reader forget it is a manual for a simulator.",
 ["Covers every subsystem procedure in one continuous concept manual.",
  "Each procedure lists concept steps plus a simulator-checkable success criterion.",
  "Emergencies route to the safe shutdown sequence, memorized by drill.",
  "A manual for a concept simulator, labeled as such on every page."],
 "Every subsystem concept; the training ladder; the shutdown sequence.",
 "console"),

(AE, "The United Concept: Apex-Reality 2.0 System Map",
 "The united concept file is the system map of the whole Apex-Reality 2.0 design: the JAH-UALNS line across the top, the AUA-JM2 protocol as the wiring, the three swarms as the body, portal theory and the reality bridge as the reach, the warp console as the hands, the memory engine as the past, and the honest labeling standard as the frame around everything. It is the one file that shows how all thirty-four concept files fit together - the map of the fiction, drawn honestly.",
 ["Maps all subsystems and their concept relationships in one view.",
  "Shows data flow: readings to the line, commands over the protocol, state into memory.",
  "Places the honest labeling standard as the frame containing the entire design.",
  "The map of a speculative design, labeled speculative on the map itself."],
 "All concept files; the honesty standard; a map layout.",
 "sheet"),
]

assert len(ENTRIES) == 34, "computer entries must be 34, got %d" % len(ENTRIES)
